{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":609.3499755859375,"text":"They are non-deterministic, which means that there is always some randomness or chance associated with what they do. In other words, you can ask it to do the same thing 10 times and it could do it 10 different ways. The second part is they have no structural separation between instructions and context. In other words, you can tell it to do something and give it some context to base its decisions off of. But if there is something in the context that conflicts with your instructions, the model isn't going to inherently prioritize your instructions because, from its point of view, all the information is coming through the same door. What you need to control is the infrastructure that your AI is running on, the environment that it is working inside. It is the difference between telling someone, don't leave a room, versus putting a lock on the door and actually using a key. The good thing about infrastructure being the problem and not the model is that you have far more control about preventing bad things from happening. So I'm not here to waste your time with vague hypotheticals or advice you can't actually act on. Like, yeah, just put the genie back in the bottle, just stop using AI. Because that's not the world we live in, right? The genie is out of the bottle. And what we have to do is to mitigate risk and harm to the best of our ability. This talk is about how you do that. Not the things that you should keep an eye out for down the line, but steps that you can take right now as soon as this week to protect yourself and your company. Before I get into the stories, though, let me set up a little bit more context. So. So. So. So. So. So. Hello again and welcome back everyone. We hope you've had a chance to take in some great conversations across the event today. As we come together for our final block of programming, please welcome back to the stage our emcee, founder, writer, and analyst at Turing Post, Ksenia Tse. Welcome back, everyone. For those who haven't met me yet, my name is Ksenia Tse. I'm writing Turing Post where we explain AI research and what it means for people to implement it in their business. So how's the conference treating you so far? All is good? Yeah? Give me some noise. Come on. Woo! Thank you. I'm sure the team appreciate that you applause to them. So let's make some more noise because it's the last leg of our journey and we have one more block of keynotes together. Let's appreciate the previous speakers and the new speakers that will come out soon. Thank you, thank you, thank you, thank you. And before we dive in, let's take a moment to thank the amazing people, the companies who supported AI Engineer. We are going to do a lot of more applause today. Thanks to them, we're able to hear so many insightful talks, meet so many incredible people, and they might become your friends, your colleagues, your partners. That's what I love about this conference. Always very meaningful connections. So this is our presenting sponsor, Erise. Please thank them. They are amazing and they made it possible. Our platinum sponsors, Oracle, Sonar, WorkOS, and AXA. Thank you, thank you, thank you. This is our gold sponsors, a huge amount of beautiful, wonderful companies that we're very thankful for. And then we have silver sponsors. Also, also a lot of applause to them. Clap, clap, clap, clap, clap. Thank you, thank you, thank you. And then basically all of us and supporting partners and every company and every speaker who made it possible. Thank you, thank you, thank you. Now a couple of words how my day went. I spent the day talking to other speakers and participants, and what I've noticed is that one of the strongest incentives to come to this conference is hiring. To the point that even some speakers are hiring and also looking for work. So I have a little idea for you about how to benefit the most from this amazing conference and make sure the financial part gets some thoughtful attention, too. So I suggest you build an agent that takes the conference program and makes a list of all the speakers and their companies, then you rank them yourself based on whose work and talk you found the most interesting and who you would actually like to work with. The next step is to send your agent to those companies' careers pages, right? And find them open positions to match them to your experience and collect the published compensation ranges. Then look at your ranking again. And if the salary changes your mind and changes your ranking, that's fine. You are updating your beliefs based on the evidence. So now while you're still here, I encourage you to go and find the people you would really like to work with or meet and talk to them. Because you've heard their talks and you know something about their work, and that would be just such a good conversation starter. And please, don't make your agents send out your resume automatically. That's basically bad karma now. So this is the project for you for the rest of the day or for the trip home. Now let's hear how AI is being put to work at Apollo. Please help me welcome Michael Manapat, co-founder of Rowspace, and Jack McDonald, principal at Apollo Global Management and CEO of Reading Ridge, for AI in Practice, an inside look","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":1.0,"end":7.0,"text":" They are non-deterministic, which means that there is always some randomness or chance associated with what they do.","tokens":[50414,814,366,2107,12,49136,259,3142,11,597,1355,300,456,307,1009,512,4974,1287,420,2931,6615,365,437,436,360,13,50714],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.23132331669330597,"compression_ratio":1.7203389406204224,"no_speech_prob":0.8235355615615845},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":7.0,"end":13.0,"text":" In other words, you can ask it to do the same thing 10 times and it could do it 10 different ways.","tokens":[50714,682,661,2283,11,291,393,1029,309,281,360,264,912,551,1266,